HUB.add({
  id: 'reactquery', title: 'React Query (TanStack Query v5)', short: 'React Query',
  blurb: 'Server state: cache, keys, staleTime vs gcTime, mutations, optimistic updates, invalidation.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is React Query, and why is it used in React applications?`,
  k: `A helper with a notebook: it fetches toys from the far shop, writes them down, and decides when the notes are too old.`,
  a: `An <b>async server-state manager</b>, not a fetching library: axios or fetch makes the call; React Query stores the result by key, shares it across components, dedupes requests, refetches stale data on triggers, retries, cancels, garbage-collects, and supports pagination, infinite queries and optimistic updates. It replaces hand-written <code>useEffect</code> fetching, which suffers race conditions, no cache, duplicate requests, stale data, no retries and leaked requests.`,
  t: `Say: "It keeps a cache of server snapshots and decides when they need refreshing." Don't copy server data into Redux or useState.` },
{ q: `How do you set up React Query in a React application?`,
  k: `Make one notebook and give it to the whole house.`,
  a: `Create one <code>QueryClient</code> in its own module with sensible defaults and wrap the app in <code>QueryClientProvider</code>; add DevTools.`,
  c: `export const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      staleTime: 2 * 60_000,
      gcTime: 10 * 60_000,
      retry: (n, err) => (err?.status >= 400 && err.status < 500 && err.status !== 429) ? false : n < 2,
    },
    mutations: { retry: 0 },
  },
})
<QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}><App /><ReactQueryDevtools /></QueryClientProvider>`,
  t: `Never new QueryClient() inside a component body: a fresh empty cache every render. With SSR, one client per request.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the useQuery hook in React Query?`,
  k: `"Please bring me this page from the notebook, and fetch it if it's missing or old."`,
  a: `Reads server data and subscribes the component (an <b>observer</b>) to one cache entry identified by <code>queryKey</code>; <code>queryFn</code> fetches it. Returns <code>data</code>, <code>status</code> (pending/success/error), <code>fetchStatus</code> (fetching/paused/idle), <code>isLoading</code> (first load), <code>isFetching</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>refetch</code>. The key must include every variable the fetch uses.`,
  c: `const { data, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['toys', 'detail', id],
  queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetchToy(id, { signal }),
  enabled: !!id,
})`,
  t: `A disabled query stays pending forever: spinner on isLoading, not isPending.` },
{ q: `How do you handle data fetching with React Query?`,
  k: `Layers: the brick shows, the helper remembers, the post office carries.`,
  a: `Component → custom hook (React Query) → service function (knows the URL) → API client (token, timeout, errors). Keep key and fetch together with <code>queryOptions</code>, pass <code>signal</code> for cancellation, return plain data, throw a typed error with <code>status</code> for retry decisions.`,
  c: `export const toyQueries = {
  list: (filters = {}) => queryOptions({
    queryKey: ['toys', 'list', filters],
    queryFn: ({ signal }) => fetchToys({ ...filters, signal }),
  }),
}
const { data = [] } = useQuery(toyQueries.list({ search }))` },
{ q: `What is the use of the QueryClient in React Query?`,
  k: `The brain that owns the notebook and can change pages directly.`,
  a: `Holds the QueryCache and MutationCache plus defaults and offers an imperative API: <code>getQueryData</code>, <code>setQueryData</code>, <code>invalidateQueries</code>, <code>prefetchQuery</code>, <code>ensureQueryData</code>, <code>cancelQueries</code>, <code>removeQueries</code>, <code>clear</code>, <code>isMutating</code>. Used in components via <code>useQueryClient</code>, and outside React (interceptors, logout, route loaders).` },
{ q: `How do you manage cache with React Query?`,
  k: `Two clocks: one says "check again?", the other says "throw it away?"`,
  a: `<code>staleTime</code> (default 0) = how long data is fresh and served without refetch; <code>gcTime</code> (default 5 min) = how long an <b>unused</b> entry stays before deletion (timer starts at 0 observers). Organise keys with factories (<code>['toys','list',filters]</code>, <code>['toys','detail',id]</code>), write known results with <code>setQueryData</code>, refresh with <code>invalidateQueries</code>, and <code>clear()</code> on logout. Pick staleTime by asking "how wrong can this screen be, and for how long?"` },
{ q: `How do you handle background data synchronization with React Query?`,
  k: `Old milk doesn't jump out of the fridge; you notice when you open the fridge. Opening the fridge is the trigger.`,
  a: `Stale is a condition, a trigger is an event; a refetch needs both. Triggers: <code>refetchOnMount</code>, <code>refetchOnWindowFocus</code>, <code>refetchOnReconnect</code>. Ignore staleness: <code>refetchInterval</code> (polling) and <code>invalidateQueries</code>. Offline queries pause (<code>networkMode: 'online'</code>). For push updates, call <code>setQueryData</code>/<code>invalidateQueries</code> from WebSocket messages.`,
  c: `refetchInterval: q => q.state.data?.status === 'delivered' ? false : 10_000` },
{ q: `How do you handle pagination with React Query?`,
  k: `Each page has its own notebook page; keep showing the old page until the new one arrives.`,
  a: `Put the page in the key and use <code>placeholderData: keepPreviousData</code> so the table doesn't flash empty; dim using <code>isPlaceholderData</code>; prefetch the next page.`,
  c: `const { data, isPlaceholderData } = useQuery({
  queryKey: ['products', 'list', { page }],
  queryFn: () => fetchProducts(page),
  placeholderData: keepPreviousData,
})` },
{ q: `How do you handle infinite scrolling with React Query?`,
  k: `The helper brings one more page each time you reach the bottom.`,
  a: `<code>useInfiniteQuery</code> with <code>initialPageParam</code> (required in v5) and <code>getNextPageParam</code> (return undefined to stop); flatten <code>data.pages</code>; call <code>fetchNextPage</code> from an IntersectionObserver sentinel when <code>hasNextPage &amp;&amp; !isFetchingNextPage</code>. Use <code>maxPages</code> to cap memory; a refetch re-fetches all loaded pages in order.`,
  c: `useInfiniteQuery({
  queryKey: ['feed'],
  queryFn: ({ pageParam }) => fetchFeed(pageParam),
  initialPageParam: null,
  getNextPageParam: last => last.nextCursor ?? undefined,
})` },
{ q: `What is the use of the useMutation hook in React Query?`,
  k: `"Please change something at the shop": add, edit or remove a toy.`,
  a: `Runs a create/update/delete only when you call <code>mutate</code>; not cached by key; <code>retry: 0</code> by default (retrying a POST can duplicate). Lifecycle: <code>onMutate</code> → <code>onSuccess</code>/<code>onError</code> → <code>onSettled</code>. The mutation hook owns invalidation; return the invalidate promise so <code>isPending</code> lasts until fresh data arrives. <code>mutate</code> never throws; <code>mutateAsync</code> does.`,
  c: `useMutation({
  mutationFn: renameOwner,
  onSuccess: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['owners'] }),
})` },
{ q: `How do you handle optimistic updates with React Query?`,
  k: `Put the toy on the shelf right away, but keep a photo of the shelf in case the shop says no.`,
  a: `In <code>onMutate</code>: <code>cancelQueries</code>, snapshot with <code>getQueryData</code>, write with <code>setQueryData</code>/<code>setQueriesData</code>, return the snapshot. <code>onError</code>: restore. <code>onSettled</code>: invalidate. Simpler v5 option: render <code>mutation.variables</code> while <code>isPending</code>. Don't be optimistic for payments or last-seat bookings.`,
  c: `onMutate: async newToy => {
  await queryClient.cancelQueries({ queryKey: ['toys', 'list'] })
  const prev = queryClient.getQueryData(['toys', 'list', {}])
  queryClient.setQueryData(['toys', 'list', {}], (old = []) => [...old, { ...newToy, id: 'temp' }])
  return { prev }
},
onError: (_e, _v, ctx) => queryClient.setQueryData(['toys', 'list', {}], ctx.prev),
onSettled: () => queryClient.invalidateQueries({ queryKey: ['toys', 'list'] }),` },
{ q: `How do you manage dependent queries in React Query?`,
  k: `"Whose bear is this?" "Emma's." "Now show Emma's other toys." Ask the second question only after the first answer.`,
  a: `Gate the second query with <code>enabled: !!value</code> (or <code>skipToken</code>). Show spinners with <code>isLoading</code>. It's a waterfall: consider a combined endpoint, a loader prefetch, or <code>initialData</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the use of the QueryCache in React Query?`,
  k: `The notebook itself: one page per name, and a list of who is reading each page.`,
  a: `The storage inside the QueryClient: a map from key hash to Query (state + observers). Two observers on one Query = one request; zero observers = inactive and the gc timer starts. Global callbacks live here since v5 removed <code>onError</code> from useQuery.`,
  c: `new QueryClient({ queryCache: new QueryCache({
  onError: (err, query) => { if (query.state.data !== undefined) toast.error('Refresh failed: ' + err.message) },
}) })` },
{ q: `How do you handle error handling with React Query?`,
  k: `The post office explains the bad news, the helper decides to try again, and the brick shows the message.`,
  a: `Normalize errors into one ApiError class (status, code) in the API client; retry by status (no retry for 4xx except 429); show <code>error</code> on first load with a retry button; one global toast for background failures via <code>QueryCache.onError</code>; <code>throwOnError</code> or <code>useSuspenseQuery</code> with Error Boundaries + <code>QueryErrorResetBoundary</code>; mutation errors mapped to form fields. During retries, status stays pending (watch <code>failureCount</code>).` },
{ q: `How do you perform query invalidation in React Query?`,
  k: `Cross out the page so the helper checks the shop again.`,
  a: `<code>invalidateQueries({ queryKey })</code> marks matching queries stale (prefix match from the left) and refetches those on screen; others refetch when next used. Options: <code>exact</code>, <code>refetchType</code> ('active' default, 'all', 'none'), <code>predicate</code>. Call it from the mutation hook and return the promise. Siblings: <code>setQueryData</code>, <code>refetchQueries</code>, <code>resetQueries</code>, <code>removeQueries</code>, <code>clear</code>.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between status and fetchStatus (isPending, isLoading, isFetching)?`,
  k: `One question asks "do I have the toy?"; the other asks "is the helper walking to the shop now?"`,
  a: `<code>status</code>: pending / success / error (do I have data?). <code>fetchStatus</code>: fetching / paused / idle (is the queryFn running?). <code>isPending</code> = no data yet; <code>isFetching</code> = running now (first load or background); <code>isLoading</code> = isPending &amp;&amp; isFetching. Big spinner on isLoading, small one on background isFetching.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between staleTime and gcTime?`,
  k: `staleTime: how long the milk is fresh. gcTime: how long an unused page stays in the notebook.`,
  a: `staleTime decides whether to refetch on a trigger (default 0, so stale immediately). gcTime decides when to delete inactive data (default 5 min, counting from 0 observers). <code>staleTime: Infinity</code> still respects invalidateQueries.` },
{ q: `What is a query key factory and queryOptions?`,
  k: `One address book for every notebook page, and a card that keeps the page name and shop directions together.`,
  a: `A key factory builds keys in levels (<code>all</code>, <code>lists()</code>, <code>list(filters)</code>, <code>details()</code>, <code>detail(id)</code>) so typos can't break invalidation and group invalidation is easy. <code>queryOptions()</code> bundles key, queryFn and options so they can't drift apart and can be reused by useQuery, prefetchQuery and getQueryData with type safety.`,
  c: `export const toyKeys = {
  all: ['toys'],
  lists: () => [...toyKeys.all, 'list'],
  list: f => [...toyKeys.lists(), f],
  detail: id => [...toyKeys.all, 'detail', id],
}` },
{ q: `What is select in React Query?`,
  k: `Count the toys in the box you already hold instead of going back to the shop.`,
  a: `<code>select</code> transforms or picks part of the cached data for one component; the cache keeps the full data. Several components can share one entry with different selects, and they only re-render when their selected value changes. Define expensive selectors outside render or memoize them.` },
{ q: `How do prefetching and placeholder data make React Query apps feel fast?`,
  k: `Open the cookie jar before the kid asks; show the old toys while new ones come.`,
  a: `<code>prefetchQuery</code> on hover, focus, or in route loaders fills the cache ahead (respects staleTime, never throws). <code>placeholderData</code> shows previous or partial data without caching it; <code>initialData</code> seeds the cache (e.g. a detail page from list data) and counts as real data.` },
{ q: `What should happen to the React Query cache on logout?`,
  k: `Wipe the notebook so the next person doesn't see your toys.`,
  a: `Call <code>queryClient.clear()</code> (and clear tokens). Invalidation is not enough: it would show the previous user's data while refetching.` },
  ],
});
